{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-88685",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Let's call $A$ the event \"Mary gets the number $1$ on the first roll,\" $B$ the event \"Mary gets the number $3$ on the second roll,\" and $C$ the event \"Mary gets the number $4$ on the third roll.\" Then, the required probability is \n\n$$\nP(A\\cap B\\cap {C}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}^{′}$ means \"Mary gets a number other than $4$ on the third roll.\"\n\nSince the rolls of a die are independent events, we can use the multiplication law for independent events, which states that\n\n$$\nP(A\\cap B\\cap {C}^{′})=P(A)\\cdot P(B)\\cdot P({C}^{′}).\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(A)=0.1$ and $P(B)=0.3.$ On the other hand, $P({C}^{′})=1-0.2=0.8.$ \n\nTherefore, substituting the above into the multiplication law for independent events, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cap B\\cap {C}^{′}) & =0.1\\cdot 0.3\\cdot 0.8 \\\\  & =0.024.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
